Block A — Environment check + scaffold (≈50 min)
A1. Confirm the stack (15 min). Run three one-liners: print python --version; import mujoco, 
gym_hil (if gym_hil errors, pip install gym_hil); and import torch; print(torch.backends.mps.is_available()).
 Done when: both imports succeed and MPS prints True. [Ref 1, Ref 6]

A2. Repo skeleton (15 min). Make a grasp-dr/ folder, git init, create empty files (harness.py, and placeholders randomize.py, 
collect_data.py, model.py, train.py, grasp_loop.py), add a one-line README title, and make your first commit. 
Done when: git status shows the tree and the initial commit exists. [Ref 5]


A3. First env contact (15–20 min). In harness.py, import gymnasium and gym_hil,
 make gym_hil/PandaPickCubeBase-v0 with image_obs=True, print "env made", env.close(). 
 Done when: it runs with no error. [Ref 1, Ref 2]


Block B — Build the harness core (≈75 min)
B1. See the observation (15 min). obs, info = env.reset();
 print obs.keys(), obs["agent_pos"].shape, and obs["pixels"]["front"].shape.
  Done when: you've seen the 18-dim state and a (128,128,3) frame. [Ref 1]
B2. One step + termination (15–20 min). Sample an action, step() once, print the five returned values. 
Then loop random steps until done or truncated, printing info and the step count at the end.
 Done when: an episode ends and you've inspected info. Note: gym-hil's documented info keys are intervention-related (is_intervention, teleop_action), so read grasp success from reward, not info — success = reward > 0 is the robust proxy. [Ref 1]
B3. Collect frames + save video (20 min). In that loop, append obs["pixels"]["front"] each step.
     Write save_video(frames, path) using imageio.mimsave. Render headless (pull from the obs, don't open the live viewer). Done when: an mp4/gif of the flailing Panda exists and plays. (The fun one.) [Ref 1, Ref 3]
B4. rollout() + evaluate() (20 min). Fold the loop into rollout(env, policy, max_steps) returning (frames, success, total_reward), with policy = lambda obs: env.action_space.sample(). Then evaluate(env, policy, n_episodes) returning success rate + mean reward. Done when: evaluate prints something like "0% success over 20 episodes." Near-zero is correct for random. [Ref 2]


Block C — Ship the artifact + lock in (≈35 min)
C1. Baseline GIF (15 min). Save one rollout as results/baseline.gif, commit it.
 Done when: the GIF is in the repo and renders on GitHub — a visible artifact on day one. [Ref 3, Ref 5]
C2. Clean up + commit (15–20 min). Add a one-line note at the top of harness.py:
 how to swap in a real policy later (any obs → action function). Commit. 
 Done when: it's committed and you can run evaluate on demand. [Ref 5]
End-of-day checkpoint: confirmed environment, a scaffolded (public-ready) repo, a harness that rolls out + evaluates + saves video, and a committed baseline GIF. Clean stopping line — Day 2 is the domain-randomization wrapper and labeled data collection, needing none of today re-run.

